First, we must inspect the code for renameCategory in src/lib/categories.ts. The function renameCategory currently:

```
export async function renameCategory(store: Store, oldName: string, newName: string): Promise<string | null> {
  // Remember the store for calls to isCategoryInUse that provide only the name.
  lastUsedStore = store;
  // Validate the new name.
  if (!isValidName(newName)) return 'Category must be 1−40 characters and not contain < or >.';
...
  // Ensure any categories present in receipts are reflected in the categories list.
  await syncCategoriesWithReceipts(store);
  // Reload after sync to get any new custom categories.
  data = await loadData(store);
...
  // Determine the canonical old name (case‑insensitive) and whether it's a default or custom category.
  const oldNameLower = oldName.toLowerCase();
...
  // Find matching default name (original key) if any.
  const defaultMatch = DEFAULT_CATEGORIES.find((def) => def.toLowerCase() === oldNameLower);
  // Find matching custom name if any.
  const customMatchIdx = data.custom.findIndex((c) => c.toLowerCase() === oldNameLower);
...
  if (!defaultMatch && customMatchIdx === -1) {
    return 'Category not found.';
  }
...
  // Compute the set of effective current category names (including renamed defaults).
  const effectiveNames = new Set<string>();
... // adding default effective rename mapping
...
  // If the new name already exists (other than the one being renamed), abort.
  const newNameLower = newName.toLowerCase();
  if (effectiveNames.has(newNameLower) && !(
    // Allow renaming to the same name with different case.
    (defaultMatch && (data.rename[defaultMatch]?.toLowerCase() === newNameLower)) ||
    (customMatchIdx !== -1 && data.custom[customMatchIdx].toLowerCase() === newNameLower)
  )) {
    return 'Category already exists.';
  }

  // Perform the rename in the stored data.
  if (defaultMatch) {
    // Record a rename from the original default name.
    data.rename[defaultMatch] = newName;
  } else {
    // Rename a custom category.
    const oldCustom = data.custom[customMatchIdx];
    data.custom[customMatchIdx] = newName;
    // Preserve any existing rename mapping for the old name.
    if (data.rename[oldCustom]) {
      data.rename[newName] = data.rename[oldCustom];
      delete data.rename[oldCustom];
    }
  }

  // Update receipts that reference the old category (case‑insensitive) atomically.
  const receipts = await getAllReceipts(store);
  const toUpdate = receipts.filter((r) => r.category && typeof r.category === 'string' && r.category.toLowerCase() === oldNameLower);
  // Keep originals for potential rollback.
  const originals = toUpdate.map((r) => ({ ...r }));
  try {
    for (const r of toUpdate) {
      const updated = { ...r, category: newName } as any;
      await store.put('receipts', updated as any);
    }
  } catch (e) {
    // Roll back any changes that succeeded before the error.
    for (const orig of originals) {
      await store.put('receipts', orig as any);
    }
    return 'Failed to rename receipts';
  }

  await saveData(store, data);
  return null;
}
```

Missing are:

- after rename, ensuring isCategoryInUse(newName) returns true (R5). That is achieved if any receipt had the oldName, then rename applied.

- ensure getAllCategories no longer lists old name (R3). Implementation: getAllCategories fetches defaults (including rename mapping) and custom categories. The function uses `rename` mapping only for defaults, but it does not filter out any rename mapping for custom categories. When a default is renamed, getAllCategories returns name: rename[def] ?? def, and id: def and hidden flag based on hidden array. It pushes these results, so the old name is not in `name`. However, in getAllCategories, the id is still def but that's internal; the name property shows new name. The requirement: "After a successful rename, getAllCategories must no longer list the old category name." It uses name property not id. So fine.

But we have to check: If a default category is renamed, defaultMatch exists; they store rename mapping. But getAllCategories uses rename mapping to assign `name: renamed`. So old name is not listed under name. However, they do not remove the default from `DEFAULT_CATEGORIES`. The id remains def, but that's used for internal representation perhaps. The function returns objects having id: def, name: renamed. So the list includes name new value. So the old name no longer appears.

If a custom category is renamed, they push custom entries from data.custom. They changed data.custom entry from old to new. So no old name appears. So R3 satisfied.

- R4: syncCategoriesWithReceipts must not re‑introduce old category name after rename. Let's examine syncCategoriesWithReceipts: it loads all receipts, loads data (custom categories etc). It adds any missing category from receipts to data.custom. The rename may cause old name still present on receipts? After rename, receipts have newName. Therefore, no old name found in receipts. However, if rename fails, receipts remain unchanged. Also if rename succeeded, receipts have newName. So syncCategoriesWithReceipts should not re-introduce oldName. That's correct.

But we must see that syncCategoriesWithReceipts runs before rename (ensuring any categories present in receipts are reflected in the list). That's okay.

Potential issue: renameCategory updates receipts via `store.put('receipts', updated)`. We need to check implementation of store.put for receipts. Let's examine src/lib/storage/store.ts.

