Potential edge case: renameCategory called with invalid newName (empty/duplicate). It checks new name valid via isValidName: length and no < >; doesn't check emptiness? It checks length >= 1. If empty string, length <1 -> false => returns error 'Category must be 1‑140 …' that is not null. In this case code returns early before syncCategoriesWithReceipts; no changes. This satisfies R6. Good.

Now validateMileageMiles code: Accepts decimal strings up to one decimal as per regex `/^\d+(\.\d)?$/`. Check logic: It first checks `if (value === '' ...) return 'Miles must be > 0';` then `const num = toNumber(value); if (isNaN(num) || num <=0) return 'Miles must be > 0';` then check > 10000; passes. Then ` const str = typeof value === 'string' ? value.trim() : num.toString(); if (!/^\d+(\.\d)?$/.test(str)) return 'Miles can have at most 1 decimal place';`. For string input `'12.5'` -> `trim` yields `'12.5'`, regex matches: `\d+` -> 12, `(\.\d)?` matches `.5`. Good. `'12.55'` fails; `'12'` matches. `'12.'` would not match because after dot no digit; so returns error. That's fine.

